Database Development Summary
Overview

As part of the continued development of AIS Elevate, a SQLite database was designed and implemented to replace hardcoded officer information with a structured, relational data model. The database serves as the application's data layer and provides persistent storage for officer records, monthly engagement evaluations, and recognition history.

The implementation focused on applying relational database design principles while preparing the application for future Java database integration using JDBC.

Database Implementation

Three relational tables were created to organize application data:

Officers stores executive officer information.
MonthlyEngagement stores monthly participation metrics used to evaluate officer engagement.
RecognitionHistory stores badge awards and recognition records for each review period.

Relationships between the tables were established using primary and foreign keys to maintain data integrity and eliminate duplicate information.

Database Testing

After creating the database structure, sample officer data was inserted into each table to verify functionality. SQL queries were executed to confirm successful data insertion, validate relationships between tables, and test the accuracy of business reports.

The completed implementation successfully generated:

Officer engagement summaries
Officer rankings based on engagement scores
Outstanding officer reports
Average organizational engagement scores
Engagement level distributions
Officer recognition history

During development, an issue involving an incorrectly configured foreign key prevented records from being inserted into the MonthlyEngagement table. The table was recreated with the appropriate foreign key relationship, resolving the issue and allowing data to be stored successfully.

Technical Skills Applied

The database implementation provided hands-on experience with relational database design and SQL development, including:

SQLite database design
Primary and foreign key relationships
Data normalization
SQL data insertion and retrieval
Multi-table joins
Filtering, sorting, and grouping data
Aggregate reporting using COUNT() and AVG()
Database testing and troubleshooting
Outcome

The successful implementation of the SQLite database represents the completion of Version 2 of the AIS Elevate prototype. The application now includes a fully functional relational database capable of storing officer information, engagement metrics, and recognition history.

The next phase of development will focus on integrating the Java application with the SQLite database using JDBC, allowing the application to retrieve, update, and manage data dynamically instead of relying on hardcoded values.
